<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malaysian New Economic Policy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Economic Policy (NEP) was an affirmative-action and development programme adopted by Malaysia in 1971, after the racial riots of 13 May 1969. It aimed to eradicate poverty and to restructure society so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumiputera">Bumiputera</a>, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, would own at least 30 per cent of corporate wealth, through preferences in education, employment and business. It formally ended in 1990, but its policies continued under successor programmes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malaysian-new-economic-policy.jpg" alt="Malaysian New Economic Policy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file: · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Malaysia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
